    sources: [{ id: "src_ogai_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "Mori Ōgai (Q356960), verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-11-02): label 'Mori Ōgai', description 'Japanese novelist and army physician (1862-1922)', born 1862-02-17, died 1922-07-08/09 (two values), Korean label 모리 오가이. Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q356960" }, { id: "src_ogai_brit", kind: "biography", title: "Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Mori Ōgai' (full article read, about 400 words). CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE: one of the creators of modern Japanese literature; studied medicine in Tokyo and in Germany 1884-88; 'Maihime' (The Dancing Girl, 1890) departed from the impersonal fiction of earlier generations and initiated a vogue for autobiographical revelation; Gan (The Wild Goose, 1911-13) his most popular novel; translated Andersen's Improvisatoren; after 1912 turned to historical fiction on the samurai code.", url: "https://www.britannica.com/biography/Mori-Ogai" }, { id: "src_ogai_ndl", kind: "institution", title: "National Diet Library, Japan, 'MORI Ogai', in the digital exhibition Portraits of Modern Japanese Historical Figures (page read in full, including the site's 'About This Site' page). Staff-written biographical note: literary man and army surgeon; University of Tokyo medical graduate 1881; study in Germany 1884-88; promoted surgeon general in 1907 and head of the Army Ministry's Medical Division, the highest post for army surgeons; head of the Imperial Museum and the Zushoryo from 1917; novelist, critic and translator while holding public office; list of representative works (Maihime, Gan, Sansho-Dayu, Takasebune, Shibue Chusai, translation Sokkyo shijin).", url: "https://www.ndl.go.jp/portrait/e/datas/342" }, { id: "src_ogai_nippon", kind: "press", title: "Otsuka Miho, 'Mori Ōgai: The Polymath Intellectual Who Made Literary History', Nippon.com, 'Shapers of Japanese History' series, 8 July 2022 (read in full, about 2,200 words). Education in Japanese, Chinese and Western learning; army surgeon from 1881; German study 1884-88 and continuing import of European publications; Omokage (1889), Maihime (1890), the magazine Shigarami-zoshi and his critical skirmishes; translations of Goethe, Andersen and Faust; the 1897 hygiene textbook; Kokura posting 1899-1902; the banned Vita Sexualis (1909); the 1910 story 'Tower of Silence'; the turn to historical stories after 1912; critical biographies of Shibue Chusai and Hojo Katei; work as head of the Imperial Household Museum and historical research on era names. Passages on family and cause of death were read and are not used.", url: "https://www.nippon.com/en/japan-topics/b07227/" }, { id: "src_ogai_hu", kind: "institution", title: "Ogai Portal, Humboldt-Universitat zu Berlin (Mori Ogai Memorial Center), 'Examining Japanese Modernity 1906-1912' (page read in full, about 2,500 words, with a dated chronology of his life 1906-1912). Staff Surgeon General and head of the Army Ministry's medical department in 1907; a Beriberi Disease Investigation Commission set up in 1908 on his suggestion with him as interim head; poetry circles and the Subaru magazine he supported; the Mukudori tsushin column on European and North American cultural and scientific trends (1909-14); the Vita Sexualis ban and reprimand; request to publish fiction under a pseudonym; his 1908 petition proposing an Academy of Arts; Ibsen's John Gabriel Borkman in his translation staged 1909; first Japanese reference to Rilke. Family events in the chronology were read and are not used.", url: "https://www.ogai.hu-berlin.de/examining-japanese-modernity" }, { id: "src_ogai_schneider", kind: "press", title: "Thomas Schneider, 'Traditionalism and Modernization: The Case of Mori Ogai', Comparative Civilizations Review 52 (Spring 2005), BYU ScholarsArchive (open-access scholarly article, first part read, about 3,000 words). Argues that Ogai's turn from fiction to history in his last decade (explained in his 1915 essay 'History as It Is and History Ignored') was a considered response to the problems of modernization rather than a retreat; draws on Kato Shuichi and J. Thomas Rimer; notes that Ogai 'has yet to achieve any satisfactory reception in the West' (Rimer). Political-science framing; discussion of nationalist slogans was read and is not scored.", url: "https://scholarsarchive.byu.edu/ccr/vol52/iss52/5/" }, { id: "src_ogai_pmc", kind: "press", title: "'Short History of Epidemiology for Noninfectious Diseases in Japan. Part 1: Selected Diseases and Related Episodes from 1880 through 1944', Journal of Epidemiology, via PubMed Central (beriberi section read, about 1,800 words). CRITICAL / COUNTER-VOICE: reports Rintaro Mori's 1888-91 assessments of the navy surgeon Takaki Kanehiro's dietary experiment (that it lacked a control group; that the army ration was nutritionally adequate), his small six-soldier diet trial, and that the army director general relied on his report and did not change rations; notes that the historian Shun'ichi Yamamoto judged Mori's reasoning physiologically defensible but not relevant to beriberi prevention. Also records that Mori coined the Japanese term for epidemiology in 1889.", url: "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7058449/" }, { id: "src_ogai_pharos", kind: "press", title: "Nakayama, beriberi in the Japanese military, The Pharos (Alpha Omega Alpha medical honor society), Autumn 2021, pp. 9-14 (PDF read in full, about 4,000 words). CRITICAL / COUNTER-VOICE: states that Rintaro Mori, as head of the Second Army medical bureau in the Sino-Japanese War, rejected requests to supply barley after the army's medical officers learned of Takaki's navy results, and that one medical officer ignored the restriction; places this inside the wider beriberi toll. Retold by a surgeon-historian, so treated as a secondary account of a disputed responsibility.", url: "https://www.alphaomegaalpha.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/11/p9-14_Nakayama_beriberi_AUT21.pdf" }, { id: "src_ogai_routledge", kind: "biography", title: "Kevin Wetmore, 'Mori, Ogai (1862-1922)', Routledge Encyclopedia of Modernism (2016), publicly visible abstract only (about 250 words; the full text is behind an institutional login and was not opened). Describes him as army surgeon, translator, novelist, dramatist and literary theorist; important for translations (especially of Ibsen) and critical writings; his print debates with his rival Tsubouchi Shoyo, who advocated Realism while Mori saw a universal ideal behind literature.", url: "https://www.rem.routledge.com/articles/mori-ogai-1862-1922" }, { id: "src_ogai_ndlbook", kind: "archive", title: "National Diet Library Digital Collection record for 'Ogai Mori Rintaro' by Mori Junzaburo (Marui Shoten, 1942), the book from which the portrait photograph was taken; the record's access restriction reads 'PDM' (Public Domain Mark). Used for the portrait only, not for any trait row.", url: "https://dl.ndl.go.jp/pid/1069406" }],
