      mastery_orientation: [68, 0.48, "s", "A"],
      // [src_zhaomf_yuanshi, src_zhaomf_siku, src_zhaomf_brit | 1279-1322 | official history, Qing editorial verdict, encyclopedia] The History of Yuan lists a commentary on the Book of Documents, the Qinyuan and Yueyuan on the origins of the zither and of music, poetry and prose, calligraphy in every script, and landscape, flower, bamboo, figure and horse painting, and quotes the historian Yang Zai that his talent was masked by calligraphy and painting, so that those who knew his painting did not know his prose; the Siku entry notes that the surviving collection includes the music treatises and that his prose stood alongside the leading Yuan writers; Britannica adds animals, landscape and bamboo. The range across calligraphy, painting, writing and music theory is documented by lists and a contemporary's remark; none of the sources reads any of the treatises, and the History of Yuan is laudatory, so confidence is moderate-low.
