    sources: [{ id: "src_zhuxi_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "Zhu Xi (Q9397), verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-11-02): English label 'Zhu Xi', description 'Chinese historian, neo-Confucian philosopher, poet, and politician during the Song dynasty (1130-1200)', Korean label 주희, birth 18 October 1130 and death 23 April 1200, occupations including philosopher, image a Qing rubbing of a stele reproducing a self-portrait. Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q9397" }, { id: "src_zhuxi_sep", kind: "biography", title: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, 'Zhu Xi' (first 100,000 of about 118,000 characters read: life, the Four Books, Reflections on Things at Hand, debates and later criticism; the final bibliography portion was not read). CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE: the preeminent Neo-Confucian (daoxue) master of the Southern Song, 'generally ranked as second only to Confucius in influence'; born in Youqi, Fujian, in 1130, passed the jinshi examination at nineteen, studied Chan Buddhism before becoming Li Tong's student in 1160, took mostly temple sinecures that left time for study, taught hundreds if not thousands of students, whose discourses are preserved in the Classified Dialogues; compiled, edited and commented on the Great Learning, Analects, Mencius and Doctrine of the Mean as the Four Books, which the Yuan dynasty adopted as the basis of the imperial examinations until 1908; the anthology Reflections on Things at Hand; debates with Lu Jiuyuan, Chen Liang, Lu Zuqian and Zhang Shi; later criticism by Wang Yangming (too outward and diffuse), Wang Fuzhi and Dai Zhen (who accused him of reading his own concepts into the classics). Scholarly reference; its praise of influence is retrospective.", url: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/zhu-xi/" }, { id: "src_zhuxi_iep", kind: "biography", title: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, 'Zhu Xi' (Neo-Confucian philosopher, 1130-1200), read in full: early life, the 1160 turn to Li Tong after an interest in Buddhism and Daoism, his teaching of 'hundreds, if not thousands' of students, the annotated editions and the Four Books (the commentary as a guide to self-cultivation, in Daniel Gardner's reading), observation guided by questions (he measured bean-sprout growth by day and by night to test a monk's claim and found no difference), the dispute with Zhang Shi over 'introspection in action' that he first accepted and later found hindered effective action, the debates with Lu Zuqian, Lu Jiuyuan and Chen Liang, and the later criticism by Wang Yangming, Wang Fuzhi and Dai Zhen. Independent scholarly reference.", url: "https://iep.utm.edu/zhu-xi/" }, { id: "src_zhuxi_songshi", kind: "archive", title: "'History of Song' (Song Shi, compiled in the Yuan dynasty), chapter 429, the biography of Zhu Xi (Chinese Wikisource, original Chinese read in full): his early studies, the teaching post at Tong'an where he selected local students and lectured daily on the Way of cultivating oneself and governing others, his poverty and simple meals shared with students who came from afar, the list of his commentaries and compilations (Yi, Odes, Great Learning and Doctrine of the Mean, Analects and Mencius commentaries, Reflections on Things at Hand), the official accusations of 'false learning' and the ban on his school during which he went on lecturing to students daily and smiled without answering those who urged him to dismiss them, his deathbed letter urging his son and students to study and revise his writings, and the Yuan-era admission of his commentaries to the schools. Written about 150 years after his death by official historians sympathetic to his school; the nearest surviving full biography. His memorials and official career in it are not used here.", url: "https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E5%AE%8B%E5%8F%B2/%E5%8D%B7429" }, { id: "src_zhuxi_chuanxilu", kind: "archive", title: "Wang Yangming, 'Instructions for Practical Living' (Chuanxilu), part 2, passage 318 (Chinese Wikisource, original Chinese read): Wang says that people say 'investigating things' should follow Zhu Xi ('Huiweng') but none had used it as he did; he and a friend tried to investigate the principle of the bamboo in front of a pavilion, the friend falling ill after three days and Wang after seven, and Wang concluded that 'there is nothing in the world that can be investigated' and that the work of investigating things lies in the mind. Critical later text from the Ming Neo-Confucian whose school opposed Zhu's method, written about three centuries after Zhu's death; used only as the counter-voice on his method of study.", url: "https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E5%82%B3%E7%BF%92%E9%8C%84/%E5%8D%B7%E4%B8%8B" }, { id: "src_zhuxi_nwe", kind: "biography", title: "New World Encyclopedia, 'Academies (Shuyuan)', read: Zhu Xi rebuilt and expanded the White Deer Grotto Academy in 1179-80 while prefect of Nankang, after it had fallen into ruin; the academy reopened in 1180 and he taught there; he set down rules that had a lasting influence on later Confucianism; it remained a major centre for about eight centuries and hosted lectures by Lu Jiuyuan, Lu Zuqian and later Wang Yangming. A tertiary encyclopedia; the dates agree with Britannica as relayed in a web search but the Britannica entry itself returned HTTP 403 and was not read.", url: "https://www.newworldencyclopedia.org/entry/Academies_%28Shuyuan%29" }],
